  <section class="mp-section" id="canadian-targeting"><div class="mp-wrap"><div class="mp-heading"><p class="mp-kicker">01 / Define the opportunity before the activity</p><h2>A useful Canadian lead<br>starts with company fit.</h2><p>Choose the account group your offer can serve, then identify the people responsible for the relevant problem.</p></div><div class="mp-scope-grid"><article><span class="mp-step">Territory</span><h3>Where can you<br>deliver well?</h3><p>Specify service areas, provinces, remote delivery and any travel limits. An account outside your operating area can look attractive and still waste a sales meeting.</p><p class="mp-output"><strong>Campaign input</strong>In-scope locations, exclusions and the salespeople who cover them.</p></article><article><span class="mp-step">Account profile</span><h3>Which businesses<br>have a reason to buy?</h3><p>Use industry, size, operating model and the problem your offer solves. Check subsidiaries and duplicate accounts before assigning contacts.</p><p class="mp-output"><strong>Campaign input</strong>Company criteria, relevant situations and a reason for exclusion.</p></article><article><span class="mp-step">Buying roles</span><h3>Who owns the<br>next conversation?</h3><p>A department title does not establish decision authority. Identify the operational owner, technical evaluator and purchasing role the campaign needs to reach.</p><p class="mp-output"><strong>Campaign input</strong>Buyer responsibilities, referral paths and qualification questions.</p></article></div>
    <aside class="mp-brief-example"><p class="mp-label">Illustrative brief / business service</p><h3>Start with a market your team can support.</h3><p>A service company covering Alberta and British Columbia might choose multi-site businesses, exclude accounts outside its delivery radius and start with operations leaders. The caller checks the current arrangement and routes a meeting only when the service need fits.</p><p>This fictional example shows how territory changes the brief. It is not a client result or a claim about a particular Canadian industry.</p></aside><div class="mp-tool-band"><div><h3>Explore a starting campaign for your market.</h3><p>Choose your offer and buyers, then use the suggested approach and resources to sharpen the brief.</p></div><a class="mp-button" href="/b2b-outbound-campaign-planner/">See How We’d Run Your Campaign ↗</a></div>
  </div></section>

  <section class="mp-section mp-paper" id="coverage-language"><div class="mp-wrap"><div class="mp-heading"><p class="mp-kicker">02 / Specify the actual delivery</p><h2>Provinces, time zones<br>and language belong in scope.</h2><p>CallTeam has a Calgary base and a globally supported operating model. Confirm the assigned people and coverage for your campaign.</p></div><div class="mp-territory-grid"><article><h3>Plan around the buyer’s local time.</h3><p>Split account groups by location. Build callbacks around the contact’s time zone, including Atlantic and Newfoundland differences where relevant, and the availability of the seller receiving the meeting.</p><p>Local holidays, shift patterns and meeting calendars can affect access. The operational plan should specify when your assigned team can call and who handles a requested callback.</p></article><article><h3>Confirm the full language path.</h3><p>This page describes English-language outreach. If Quebec or another segment requires French, confirm capability for the opening conversation, objections, written follow-up and sales meeting before agreeing the engagement.</p><p>A Canadian office does not establish French-language delivery or Canadian-only callers. Put any language and caller-location requirements into the proposal.</p></article></div>
    <p>Agree the source and permitted use of account data, CRM access, exports, ownership terms and stop-request handling. For Canadian business calls, confirm the relevant registration and contact requirements; review proposed email or text follow-up separately.</p><p>The <a href="/services/b2b-telemarketing/#contact-controls">telemarketing contact-control overview</a> links to current regulator guidance. The <a href="/articles/b2b-cold-calling-compliance-global/">global compliance guide</a> provides the broader planning framework.</p>
  </div></section>

  <section class="mp-section" id="cross-border"><div class="mp-wrap mp-split"><div><p class="mp-kicker">03 / Selling from Canada into the United States</p><h2>Give each market<br>its own campaign view.</h2><p class="mp-section-lead">Canadian and US accounts can share a product offer while needing different account selection, buyer language and follow-up.</p><p>Separate the target groups, selling regions, relevant proof, call schedules and commercial questions. Confirm which seller receives each meeting and how any currency or contracting question is answered.</p><p>Keep response and opportunity reporting separate enough to see which territory is progressing. Strong results in one segment should lead to a specific decision about the next, rather than an automatic national expansion.</p><p>Testing a new American segment? Our <a href="/services/us-market-entry-sales/">US market entry sales service</a> covers that deeper market-entry assignment.</p></div><aside class="mp-note"><p class="mp-label">Illustrative Canadian or US sales handoff</p><h3>Let the seller prepare<br>before the meeting.</h3><dl><div><dt>Territory</dt><dd>Account location, service eligibility and local time zone.</dd></div><div><dt>Buyer</dt><dd>Responsibility for the issue and the role of other stakeholders.</dd></div><div><dt>Reason</dt><dd>Business situation and why a conversation was accepted.</dd></div><div><dt>Open items</dt><dd>Questions still needing discovery, including timing and approval.</dd></div><div><dt>Next action</dt><dd>Meeting purpose, assigned seller and follow-up commitment.</dd></div></dl><p class="mp-small">Example fields, tailored to the agreed campaign and CRM.</p></aside></div></section>

